<article class="env-definition" id="E864AB">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/E864AB">Definition <span data-tag="E864AB">5.5.4.5</span>.</a>strongly saturatedsaturated!strongly Let $\calC $ be a $\infty $-category which admits small colimits, and let $S$ be a collection of morphisms of $\calC $. We will say that $S$ is <em>strongly saturated</em> if it satisfies the following conditions: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
Given a pushout diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { C \ar [r]^{f} \ar [d] &amp;  D \ar [d] \\ C' \ar [r]^{f'} \ar [r] &amp;  D' } \]
</div>
<p>
 in $\calC $, if $f$ belongs to $S$, then so does $f'$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
The full subcategory of $\Fun (\Delta ^1, \calC )$ spanned by $S$ is stable under small colimits. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
Suppose given a $2$-simplex of $\calC $, corresponding to a diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { X \ar [rr]^{f} \ar [dr]^{g} &amp;  &amp;  Y \ar [dl]_{h} \\ &amp;  Z. &amp;  } \]
</div>
<p>
 If any two of $f$, $g$, and $h$ belong to $S$, then so does the third. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
